<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · island</p><h1 class="enc-title">Trinidad</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Trinidad is the larger of the two main islands of Trinidad and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tobago">Tobago</a>, lying just off the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orinoco">Orinoco</a> delta of Venezuela. Sighted by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christopher-columbus">Columbus</a> in 1498, it remained a thinly settled Spanish colony until French Catholic planters arrived after 1783, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> captured it in 1797. Indian indentured labour after emancipation, oil and asphalt shaped its economy, and it became independent together with Tobago in 1962.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/trinidad.jpg" alt="Trinidad" width="320" height="344" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: CIA · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Td-map.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
